1
00:00:00,320 --> 00:00:03,809
Inference questions end with
a blank and one question:

2
00:00:03,960 --> 00:00:07,690
which choice most logically
completes the text?

3
00:00:07,920 --> 00:00:11,500
A common mistake is picking
an answer that could be true.

4
00:00:11,520 --> 00:00:14,340
The right answer is the
one that has to be true,

5
00:00:14,360 --> 00:00:15,930
based on the text.

6
00:00:16,389 --> 00:00:19,449
In this video: what these
questions are really testing,

7
00:00:19,469 --> 00:00:23,809
a three-step method, six
original SAT-style questions,

8
00:00:23,829 --> 00:00:26,919
and the four trap answers
you'll keep running into.

9
00:00:27,378 --> 00:00:32,038
An inference question gives you a
short passage that builds an argument.

10
00:00:32,058 --> 00:00:34,998
A few facts, a claim, maybe a contrast.

11
00:00:35,018 --> 00:00:37,837
Then the last sentence stops halfway.

12
00:00:37,857 --> 00:00:40,468
Your job is to finish the reasoning.

13
00:00:40,916 --> 00:00:43,336
Think of it like the last line of a proof.

14
00:00:43,356 --> 00:00:45,976
The lines above it have to lead there.

15
00:00:45,996 --> 00:00:49,926
The answer isn't a guess
about what else might be true,

16
00:00:50,155 --> 00:00:54,086
and it isn't something you happen
to know from outside the passage.

17
00:00:54,539 --> 00:00:59,039
These are Inference questions, part
of the Information and Ideas domain,

18
00:00:59,059 --> 00:01:03,199
which is about a quarter of the
Reading and Writing section.

19
00:01:03,219 --> 00:01:05,758
Reading and Writing
passages come from science,

20
00:01:05,778 --> 00:01:08,678
history and social
studies, the humanities,

21
00:01:08,698 --> 00:01:09,909
and literature.

22
00:01:10,373 --> 00:01:12,343
They're easy to recognize.

23
00:01:12,493 --> 00:01:15,393
The blank is at the
very end of the passage,

24
00:01:15,413 --> 00:01:18,193
and the question is
almost always the same:

25
00:01:18,213 --> 00:01:21,743
which choice most logically
completes the text?

26
00:01:22,221 --> 00:01:24,361
Now look at the words
right before the blank.

27
00:01:24,381 --> 00:01:27,311
They tell you what kind
of ending you need.

28
00:01:27,501 --> 00:01:31,200
Therefore, so, or thus means a conclusion.

29
00:01:31,220 --> 00:01:34,071
However or but means a contrast.

30
00:01:34,261 --> 00:01:37,721
Suggests or most likely
means a careful conclusion,

31
00:01:37,741 --> 00:01:39,311
not a certain one.

32
00:01:39,769 --> 00:01:40,819
Here's the method.

33
00:01:40,969 --> 00:01:43,579
Step one: trace the logic.

34
00:01:43,648 --> 00:01:47,139
Mark each claim, and how it
connects to the next one.

35
00:01:47,169 --> 00:01:48,459
Is it a cause?

36
00:01:48,569 --> 00:01:49,939
A contrast?

37
00:01:49,969 --> 00:01:52,498
A condition, like only if?

38
00:01:52,956 --> 00:01:54,926
Step two: predict.

39
00:01:55,355 --> 00:01:59,286
Before you look at the choices,
finish the sentence in your own words.

40
00:01:59,396 --> 00:02:01,415
A rough prediction is fine.

41
00:02:01,435 --> 00:02:03,886
It keeps the choices from steering you.

42
00:02:04,319 --> 00:02:08,129
Step three: choose the
answer the text guarantees.

43
00:02:08,399 --> 00:02:11,939
For each choice, ask: does
the passage force this?

44
00:02:11,959 --> 00:02:15,778
If it needs an extra fact, or a
bigger leap than the passage makes,

45
00:02:15,798 --> 00:02:16,808
it's out.

46
00:02:17,263 --> 00:02:18,323
Question one.

47
00:02:18,343 --> 00:02:22,043
Tardigrades, tiny animals
often found in moss,

48
00:02:22,063 --> 00:02:25,123
can survive being completely dried out.

49
00:02:25,143 --> 00:02:28,283
As they lose water, they
curl into a compact,

50
00:02:28,303 --> 00:02:33,683
dormant form called a tun, in which
their metabolism nearly stops.

51
00:02:33,703 --> 00:02:38,433
When water returns, a tun can
rehydrate and become active again.

52
00:02:38,463 --> 00:02:42,563
A tardigrade that looks lifeless
in a sample of dried moss,

53
00:02:42,583 --> 00:02:44,513
then, blank

54
00:02:45,045 --> 00:02:46,455
Pause and try it.

55
00:02:52,261 --> 00:02:53,121
Trace it.

56
00:02:53,141 --> 00:02:55,841
Dried out tardigrades become tuns.

57
00:02:55,861 --> 00:02:59,201
Tuns can become active
again when water returns.

58
00:02:59,221 --> 00:03:04,150
So a tardigrade that looks dead
in dry moss might just be a tun.

59
00:03:04,420 --> 00:03:07,191
Prediction: it could still be alive.

60
00:03:07,621 --> 00:03:12,401
Choice B: may still be alive, and could
become active again if the moss is

61
00:03:12,421 --> 00:03:12,921
moistened.

62
00:03:12,941 --> 00:03:14,561
That's the answer.

63
00:03:14,581 --> 00:03:16,401
Notice the word may.

64
00:03:16,421 --> 00:03:19,680
The passage says tardigrades
can survive drying out,

65
00:03:19,700 --> 00:03:21,281
not that every one does.

66
00:03:21,301 --> 00:03:23,711
So a careful answer is the right fit.

67
00:03:24,216 --> 00:03:27,636
A gets the logic backward: it
says the tardigrade has died.

68
00:03:27,656 --> 00:03:31,116
D is too strong:
certainly, within minutes.

69
00:03:31,136 --> 00:03:32,956
And C is the interesting one.

70
00:03:32,976 --> 00:03:36,595
Tardigrades really have survived
exposure to outer space.

71
00:03:36,615 --> 00:03:39,796
But the passage never mentions space.

72
00:03:39,816 --> 00:03:43,786
True in real life is not the
same as supported by the text.

73
00:03:44,316 --> 00:03:45,416
Question two.

74
00:03:45,436 --> 00:03:50,366
The Venus flytrap, a plant native to
wetlands in North and South Carolina,

75
00:03:50,396 --> 00:03:53,686
catches insects with
leaves that snap shut.

76
00:03:53,875 --> 00:03:58,816
Each leaf has several trigger hairs,
and a leaf closes only if its hairs are

77
00:03:58,836 --> 00:04:02,126
touched at least twice
within about twenty seconds.

78
00:04:02,588 --> 00:04:07,928
An insect crawling across a leaf usually
brushes the hairs more than once,

79
00:04:07,948 --> 00:04:13,278
but a raindrop or a falling seed
typically touches a hair just once.

80
00:04:13,308 --> 00:04:17,178
This requirement therefore
most likely blank

81
00:04:17,613 --> 00:04:19,023
Pause and try it.

82
00:04:24,828 --> 00:04:25,828
Trace it.

83
00:04:25,868 --> 00:04:28,727
Insects usually touch
the hairs more than once.

84
00:04:28,747 --> 00:04:31,488
Raindrops and seeds typically touch once.

85
00:04:31,508 --> 00:04:33,088
A leaf needs two touches.

86
00:04:33,108 --> 00:04:37,367
So the rule filters out most of
the things that aren't insects.

87
00:04:37,387 --> 00:04:40,518
Prediction: fewer false alarms.

88
00:04:40,965 --> 00:04:46,905
Choice D: it reduces how often the plant
closes a leaf on objects that are not

89
00:04:46,925 --> 00:04:47,665
prey.

90
00:04:47,685 --> 00:04:48,785
Reduces.

91
00:04:48,805 --> 00:04:50,985
That's exactly as strong as the passage,

92
00:04:51,005 --> 00:04:52,145
and no stronger.

93
00:04:52,165 --> 00:04:53,655
D is the answer.

94
00:04:54,093 --> 00:04:59,313
Now compare A: it prevents the plant from
ever closing on anything other than a

95
00:04:59,333 --> 00:05:00,623
living insect.

96
00:05:00,813 --> 00:05:01,553
Ever?

97
00:05:01,573 --> 00:05:06,193
The passage says raindrops and
seeds typically touch a hair once.

98
00:05:06,213 --> 00:05:07,993
Typically isn't always.

99
00:05:08,013 --> 00:05:09,703
That's the too strong trap.

100
00:05:09,743 --> 00:05:16,363
Words like ever, always, never, all,
and proves need a passage that's just as

101
00:05:16,383 --> 00:05:17,473
absolute.

102
00:05:18,046 --> 00:05:20,615
B and C are out of scope.

103
00:05:20,646 --> 00:05:24,266
The passage says nothing about
how fast the leaves close,

104
00:05:24,286 --> 00:05:27,256
or how many insects live in the wetlands.

105
00:05:27,664 --> 00:05:28,964
Question three.

106
00:05:28,984 --> 00:05:34,154
When the price of a good rises,
people generally buy less of it.

107
00:05:34,424 --> 00:05:40,643
Economists call demand for a good
inelastic when the percentage drop in the

108
00:05:40,663 --> 00:05:45,394
quantity people buy is smaller
than the percentage rise in price.

109
00:05:45,864 --> 00:05:48,393
Studies have found that in the short run,

110
00:05:48,424 --> 00:05:52,284
demand for gasoline in the
United States is inelastic,

111
00:05:52,304 --> 00:05:57,154
in part because many drivers can't
quickly change how far they need to drive.

112
00:05:57,174 --> 00:06:01,794
If gasoline prices rose by
ten percent over a few weeks,

113
00:06:01,814 --> 00:06:08,094
economists would therefore expect the
quantity of gasoline people buy to blank

114
00:06:08,561 --> 00:06:09,971
Pause and try it.

115
00:06:16,777 --> 00:06:17,777
Trace it.

116
00:06:17,817 --> 00:06:20,547
Price goes up, so people buy less.

117
00:06:20,696 --> 00:06:21,677
That's a drop.

118
00:06:21,697 --> 00:06:26,756
Inelastic means the percent drop is
smaller than the percent rise in price.

119
00:06:26,776 --> 00:06:29,557
Gas is inelastic in the short run.

120
00:06:29,577 --> 00:06:31,597
Prices rise ten percent.

121
00:06:31,617 --> 00:06:34,917
So the quantity falls, but
by less than ten percent.

122
00:06:34,937 --> 00:06:36,267
That's choice C.

123
00:06:36,766 --> 00:06:39,505
A says the quantity falls
by more than ten percent.

124
00:06:39,525 --> 00:06:43,226
That's the definition of
inelastic turned upside down.

125
00:06:43,246 --> 00:06:45,346
This is the reversed logic trap.

126
00:06:45,366 --> 00:06:49,776
It uses the right words, but the
relationship runs the wrong way.

127
00:06:50,316 --> 00:06:53,615
B goes too far: stay exactly the same.

128
00:06:53,635 --> 00:06:58,166
Can't quickly change isn't the
same as can't change at all,

129
00:06:58,236 --> 00:07:01,736
and the passage says
people generally buy less.

130
00:07:01,756 --> 00:07:04,576
And nothing in the passage supports D,

131
00:07:04,596 --> 00:07:05,965
the quantity going up.

132
00:07:06,447 --> 00:07:07,737
Question four.

133
00:07:07,967 --> 00:07:13,227
In the eighteen eighties, the French
painters Georges Seurat and Paul Signac

134
00:07:13,247 --> 00:07:16,826
developed pointillism, a
technique in which small,

135
00:07:16,846 --> 00:07:20,537
distinct dots of color
are applied to a canvas.

136
00:07:20,557 --> 00:07:23,616
Rather than mixing every
shade on a palette,

137
00:07:23,636 --> 00:07:26,527
pointillists relied on the viewer's eye:

138
00:07:26,557 --> 00:07:31,337
from a distance, neighboring dots of
different colors can appear to blend into a

139
00:07:31,357 --> 00:07:32,607
single tone.

140
00:07:33,147 --> 00:07:37,006
This effect, however, depends
on the dots being too small,

141
00:07:37,026 --> 00:07:41,317
from where the viewer stands,
to be seen as separate marks.

142
00:07:41,587 --> 00:07:47,007
A viewer standing just a few inches from
a pointillist painting would therefore

143
00:07:47,027 --> 00:07:49,437
most likely blank

144
00:07:49,896 --> 00:07:51,306
Pause and try it.

145
00:07:58,112 --> 00:07:59,052
Trace it.

146
00:07:59,072 --> 00:08:03,892
The blending only happens when the dots
are too small to see as separate marks.

147
00:08:03,912 --> 00:08:06,812
A few inches away, they
aren't too small anymore.

148
00:08:06,832 --> 00:08:08,841
So the blending breaks down.

149
00:08:09,032 --> 00:08:11,932
Prediction: you'd see the separate dots.

150
00:08:11,952 --> 00:08:13,402
That's choice A.

151
00:08:13,823 --> 00:08:17,803
B reverses it: the colors
blend even more up close.

152
00:08:17,823 --> 00:08:19,763
C is out of scope.

153
00:08:19,783 --> 00:08:22,603
It compares pointillism
with Impressionist painting,

154
00:08:22,623 --> 00:08:24,362
which the passage never mentions.

155
00:08:24,382 --> 00:08:27,632
It sounds like art history,
so it feels relevant.

156
00:08:27,663 --> 00:08:30,723
But the passage gives you
nothing to judge it with.

157
00:08:30,743 --> 00:08:32,713
That's the out of scope trap.

158
00:08:33,091 --> 00:08:37,791
And D, unable to tell what
the painting shows at all,

159
00:08:37,811 --> 00:08:39,271
is too strong.

160
00:08:39,291 --> 00:08:43,181
Seeing separate dots doesn't mean
you can't recognize anything.

161
00:08:43,565 --> 00:08:44,985
So those are the four traps.

162
00:08:45,005 --> 00:08:48,625
Too strong: the answer claims
more than the passage can prove.

163
00:08:48,645 --> 00:08:53,175
Out of scope: it's about something
the passage never discusses.

164
00:08:53,245 --> 00:08:55,864
Reversed: it flips the relationship.

165
00:08:55,884 --> 00:08:58,385
And true in real life: it might be a fact,

166
00:08:58,405 --> 00:09:00,175
but it isn't in the text.

167
00:09:00,715 --> 00:09:04,135
On harder questions, the
logic takes an extra step,

168
00:09:04,155 --> 00:09:06,095
and the traps get harder to see.

169
00:09:06,115 --> 00:09:07,205
Here are two.

170
00:09:07,634 --> 00:09:08,934
Question five.

171
00:09:08,954 --> 00:09:13,654
Dendrochronologists, scientists
who date wood using tree rings,

172
00:09:13,674 --> 00:09:19,013
match a sample's pattern of wide and
narrow rings to a master record built from

173
00:09:19,033 --> 00:09:24,244
trees of known age, which lets them
assign a calendar year to each ring.

174
00:09:24,690 --> 00:09:27,549
If a beam still has its outermost ring,

175
00:09:27,569 --> 00:09:31,110
the last one the tree grew
before it was cut down,

176
00:09:31,130 --> 00:09:33,909
researchers can tell
when the tree was felled.

177
00:09:33,929 --> 00:09:36,909
But a beam's outer
rings are often missing,

178
00:09:36,929 --> 00:09:38,780
trimmed away when the beam was shaped.

179
00:09:39,290 --> 00:09:44,270
So if a trimmed beam's outermost
surviving ring dates to twelve fifty,

180
00:09:44,290 --> 00:09:48,940
researchers can conclude only
that the tree was cut down blank

181
00:09:49,377 --> 00:09:50,787
Pause and try it.

182
00:09:58,593 --> 00:09:59,893
Trace it carefully.

183
00:09:59,913 --> 00:10:04,933
The passage tells you the outermost
ring is the last one the tree grew.

184
00:10:04,953 --> 00:10:07,293
So the outer rings are the newest.

185
00:10:07,313 --> 00:10:10,493
On this beam, some outer
rings were trimmed off.

186
00:10:10,513 --> 00:10:13,563
Those missing rings
grew after twelve fifty.

187
00:10:13,975 --> 00:10:16,955
So the tree was still
alive in twelve fifty,

188
00:10:16,975 --> 00:10:20,755
and it may have kept growing
for years after that.

189
00:10:20,775 --> 00:10:24,985
It was cut down in twelve
fifty at the earliest.

190
00:10:25,214 --> 00:10:28,594
Prediction: no earlier than twelve fifty.

191
00:10:28,614 --> 00:10:30,065
That's choice C.

192
00:10:30,514 --> 00:10:34,014
B is the reversed trap: no
later than twelve fifty.

193
00:10:34,034 --> 00:10:36,364
Same number, logic flipped.

194
00:10:36,514 --> 00:10:37,854
A is too strong.

195
00:10:37,874 --> 00:10:43,214
Exactly twelve fifty would only work
if the outermost ring were still there.

196
00:10:43,234 --> 00:10:48,614
And D sounds reasonable, but the passage
says nothing about when beams were

197
00:10:48,634 --> 00:10:49,634
installed.

198
00:10:50,014 --> 00:10:50,754
Last one.

199
00:10:50,774 --> 00:10:51,904
The hardest.

200
00:10:52,293 --> 00:10:57,624
Ice cores drilled in Antarctica
contain tiny bubbles of ancient air.

201
00:10:57,654 --> 00:11:00,553
Each year's snow buries
the snow beneath it,

202
00:11:00,573 --> 00:11:03,824
slowly compressing older layers into ice.

203
00:11:03,844 --> 00:11:08,184
Near the surface, though, air still
moves between the grains of packed snow,

204
00:11:08,204 --> 00:11:13,184
and pockets of air aren't sealed off
from the atmosphere until they're roughly

205
00:11:13,204 --> 00:11:15,864
forty to one hundred twenty meters deep.

206
00:11:15,884 --> 00:11:20,704
As a result, the air
trapped in a bubble is blank

207
00:11:21,143 --> 00:11:22,363
Pause and try it.

208
00:11:22,383 --> 00:11:24,073
Take a little longer on this one.

209
00:11:32,876 --> 00:11:33,696
Trace it.

210
00:11:33,716 --> 00:11:36,215
Each year's snow buries the snow below it.

211
00:11:36,235 --> 00:11:41,095
So snow that's forty to one hundred
twenty meters down has been buried for a

212
00:11:41,115 --> 00:11:41,696
long time.

213
00:11:41,716 --> 00:11:42,696
It's old.

214
00:11:42,716 --> 00:11:47,296
But the air between its grains kept
mixing with the atmosphere the whole time.

215
00:11:47,316 --> 00:11:50,166
The air only gets locked
in at the very end.

216
00:11:50,595 --> 00:11:56,085
So when a bubble finally seals,
the ice around it is old,

217
00:11:56,115 --> 00:11:59,054
and the air inside it is newer.

218
00:11:59,074 --> 00:12:02,335
The air is younger than
the ice that surrounds it.

219
00:12:02,355 --> 00:12:04,084
The answer is D.

220
00:12:04,500 --> 00:12:08,319
A is the reversed trap:
older than the ice.

221
00:12:08,339 --> 00:12:12,400
It sounds right, because the
passage calls it ancient air.

222
00:12:12,420 --> 00:12:15,510
But trace the steps, and it's backward.

223
00:12:15,966 --> 00:12:18,146
And C is true in real life.

224
00:12:18,166 --> 00:12:23,666
Scientists really do use the air in
ice cores to study past carbon dioxide

225
00:12:23,686 --> 00:12:24,686
levels.

226
00:12:24,965 --> 00:12:28,226
But the blank starts with as a result.

227
00:12:28,246 --> 00:12:31,345
The answer has to follow
from that sealing delay,

228
00:12:31,365 --> 00:12:32,906
and C doesn't.

229
00:12:32,926 --> 00:12:37,856
The words before the blank tell
you what job the answer has to do.

230
00:12:38,318 --> 00:12:40,088
Here's your test-day routine.

231
00:12:40,318 --> 00:12:45,168
Read the passage once, tracing how
each sentence connects to the next.

232
00:12:45,278 --> 00:12:48,777
Then look hard at the words
right before the blank.

233
00:12:48,797 --> 00:12:53,418
They tell you the job: a
conclusion, a contrast,

234
00:12:53,438 --> 00:12:55,048
or a careful maybe.

235
00:12:55,496 --> 00:12:58,625
Predict the ending before
you look at the choices.

236
00:12:58,775 --> 00:13:02,076
Then test each choice with one question:

237
00:13:02,096 --> 00:13:04,065
does the text force this?

238
00:13:04,256 --> 00:13:07,276
Cross out anything too
strong, out of scope,

239
00:13:07,296 --> 00:13:10,466
reversed, or true only in real life.

240
00:13:10,946 --> 00:13:13,205
Match the strength of the passage.

241
00:13:13,225 --> 00:13:18,885
If it says may, often, or typically,
the right answer will usually be just as

242
00:13:18,905 --> 00:13:19,646
careful.

243
00:13:19,666 --> 00:13:23,956
An absolute word in a
choice is a warning sign.

244
00:13:24,403 --> 00:13:29,783
On average, you have a little over a
minute per Reading and Writing question.

245
00:13:29,803 --> 00:13:34,333
These passages can be dense, so
don't reread the whole thing.

246
00:13:34,882 --> 00:13:37,773
Reread the sentence
that sets up the blank.

247
00:13:38,256 --> 00:13:39,156
Recap.

248
00:13:39,176 --> 00:13:44,636
Trace the logic, predict the ending, and
